<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelopidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 364 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelopidas was a Theban general and statesman who led the exiles who freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> from its Spartan garrison in 379 BCE. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> he revived the Boeotian League, commanded the Sacred Band at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE and helped make Thebes the leading power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. He was killed in 364 BCE at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a>, fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-pherae">Alexander of Pherae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelopidas.jpg" alt="Pelopidas" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrey Ivanov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivanov,_Andrey_-_Pelopidas%27s_death_-_1805-1806.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pelopidas came from a distinguished family and had great wealth, which he spent on his friends and on public service while he lived the rough life of an athlete. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> he took care of the deserving poor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and was ashamed to spend more on himself than the poorest Thebans did. When friends urged him to look after his money for the sake of his wife and children, he pointed to a blind, crippled pauper named Nicodemus and said that money was necessary for Nicodemus.</p><p>In 384 BCE he served in a Theban contingent sent to support the Spartans at the Siege of Mantinea, where the Arcadians wounded him seven times in front. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> defended his body and arms single-handed until the Spartan king Agesipolis came to their aid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says the incident cemented their friendship, and Pelopidas was Epaminondas's partner in politics for the next twenty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Spartans seized the Theban citadel in 382 BCE, Pelopidas fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and led a conspiracy to free the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> installed oligarchic governments in the cities it conquered. In 379 BCE his democratic faction rose in a surprise revolt, killed many of the aristocrats who supported Spartan rule, and the Spartan garrison surrendered to the army he gathered.</p><p>He was elected boeotarch, or war leader, in that year and in twelve later ones. Around 375 BCE he routed a much larger Spartan force at Tegyra, a victory owed mainly to the Sacred Band, an elite corps of 300 soldiers. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE he made the Sacred Band close with the Spartans so quickly that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>'s new oblique order succeeded, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> began to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> as the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In 370 BCE both generals entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and refounded Messene as an independent city, which permanently deprived Sparta of its hegemonic power. In 367 BCE Pelopidas went as envoy to the Persian king Artaxerxes II and obtained a settlement of Greece to Theban wishes, including the independence of Messene.</p><p>In 369 BCE he marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-pherae">Alexander of Pherae</a> at the request of the Thessalians and then arbitrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> between two claimants to the throne, bringing hostages home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, among them the king's younger brother Philip, later king himself. Alexander of Pherae later seized him, and two expeditions from Thebes were needed to free him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 364 BCE the Thessalian towns appealed again against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-pherae">Alexander of Pherae</a>. An eclipse of the sun limited the troops Pelopidas could bring, yet he defeated the tyrant's larger force on the ridge of Cynoscephalae. Wishing to kill Alexander himself, he rushed forward and was cut down by the tyrant's guards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> considered him a leader who threw away his life through recklessness and anger.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelopidas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 378 BCE Thebes formed the Sacred Band, an elite unit of 300 men, and <b>Pelopidas</b> used it to defeat a larger Spartan force at the Battle of Tegyra in 375 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Trained and led by Epaminondas and <b>Pelopidas</b>, with the Sacred Band of Thebes, the Theban army won at Leuctra in 371 BC, then carried its arms into the Peloponnesus, freeing many helots, and sent expeditions to Thessaly and Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sparta seized the Kadmeia in 382 BCE, but <b>Pelopidas</b> liberated it in 379 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander of Pherae (369-358 BCE) was notorious for cruelty and fought Thebes, killing <b>Pelopidas</b> at Cynoscephalae in 364 BCE though losing the battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tyranny-of-pherae">Tyranny of Pherae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,23.32&amp;year=-374" aria-label="Thebes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5130 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pelopidas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,23.32&amp;year=-374">What was here in 374 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
